1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:02,610
At 7:42 this morning,

2
00:00:02,640 --> 00:00:05,290
a man named Dev honked his horn.

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:10,450
It took him half a second. By eight a.m.,

4
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:12,810
it will reach a nine-year-old he will never meet.

5
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:17,490
This is the butterfly effect… of a bad mood.

6
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:22,290
Meet Maya. Maya was having a perfectly nice morning.

7
00:00:22,320 --> 00:00:25,770
Then a car horn took about eleven years off her life.

8
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:28,130
She didn't know Dev. Didn't matter.

9
00:00:28,160 --> 00:00:30,730
His bad mood was hers now. Free of charge.

10
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:35,570
Degree one. Maya walks into her favorite café,

11
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:37,530
carrying something she never ordered.

12
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:44,590
Leo the barista had been in a great mood.

13
00:00:44,620 --> 00:00:45,270
Had.

14
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:50,590
Psychologists call this emotional contagion. In one study,

15
00:00:50,620 --> 00:00:55,390
people who got a rude partner were later rated as rude by their next partner.

16
00:00:55,420 --> 00:00:57,990
Even when that next meeting came a week later.

17
00:00:58,500 --> 00:00:59,910
Rudeness, it turns out,

18
00:00:59,940 --> 00:01:02,190
is a cold you catch from strangers.

19
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:06,270
Degree two. Enter Sam. Nine years old,

20
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:09,190
paying for a hot chocolate entirely in coins.

21
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:10,300
[Leo] NEXT!

22
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:14,590
Degree three. Three people. Eleven minutes. One horn.

23
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:18,070
And this is usually where the story ends.

24
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:19,590
The mood keeps rolling,

25
00:01:19,620 --> 00:01:21,950
and nobody ever finds out where it started.

26
00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:26,830
But Sam was about to do something nine-year-olds are weirdly good at.

27
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:33,390
Instead of passing the bad mood on,

28
00:01:33,420 --> 00:01:35,190
Sam passed on something else.

29
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:38,750
This is the ripple effect. Running backwards.

30
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:41,550
Leo cracked first.

31
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:45,870
Maya cracked second.

32
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:48,870
And at the crosswalk, Maya waves a car through.

33
00:01:48,900 --> 00:01:49,870
Guess whose.

34
00:01:50,700 --> 00:01:52,190
Dev wasn't a monster.

35
00:01:52,220 --> 00:01:54,790
He was late for his daughter's first school play.

36
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:57,990
She was playing the butterfly. Obviously.

37
00:02:00,100 --> 00:02:04,790
One study followed four thousand seven hundred people for twenty years.

38
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:07,190
When someone you're directly connected to is happy,

39
00:02:07,220 --> 00:02:10,070
you're about fifteen percent more likely to be happy too.

40
00:02:10,100 --> 00:02:13,990
Their friends: almost ten percent. Their friends' friends:

41
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:14,990
about six.

42
00:02:15,700 --> 00:02:18,310
Three degrees. Strangers you'll never meet,

43
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:20,030
feeling a little of what you feel.

44
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:28,350
It's how one video by MrBeast and Mark Rober got millions of strangers to pay for twenty million trees.

45
00:02:30,300 --> 00:02:33,150
You'll never meet most of the people your mood reaches.

46
00:02:33,180 --> 00:02:35,350
But every one of them will meet your mood.

47
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:38,250
So today, pass on a good one.

48
00:02:38,280 --> 00:02:40,650
Hold a door. Draw a terrible picture.

49
00:02:40,680 --> 00:02:42,130
Let someone cross.

50
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:45,890
Then tell us: who's in your three degrees?

51
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:47,810
And if you spotted the butterfly…

52
00:02:47,840 --> 00:02:49,130
you know where to put it.

